{
  "$comment": "C 7.8 <inttypes.h> — an OS/libc surface. ★ P69 (lane lm, D-FFI-INTTYPES-H-SHIPS-FOUR-FORMAT-MACROS): the WHOLE header — every PRI/SCN macro for the 8/16/32/64-bit, LEAST, FAST, MAX and PTR widths, C23's binary ones, imaxdiv_t, and imaxabs, imaxdiv, strtoimax, strtoumax, wcstoimax and wcstoumax. It shipped four macros (PRIi64, PRIu64, PRIi32, PRIu32) with their modifiers RESTATED per format (TF-C66, need-driven for the sqlite testfixture); each is now an alias of the type-format predefine, so no modifier is stated here at all. ✔MEASURED: ucrtbase.dll exports all six functions (its export table: imaxabs 2231, imaxdiv 2232, strtoimax 2405, strtoumax 2413, wcstoimax 2465, wcstoumax 2475), Apple clang 21 binds the plain names on both arches, and glibc 2.39 under C23 binds the four conversions to their C23 entry points `__isoc23_<name>` @GLIBC_2.38 (the load floor and the other ten names: stdlib.json's `$isoc23EntryPointsComment`). imaxabs/imaxdiv are each C library's own. The four conversions are C23's on every pair: glibc's own entry points on elf; on pe and macho, whose libraries answer strtoimax(\"0b101\", 0, 0) with 0, DSS's `__dss_isoc23_<name>` (runtime/platform/src/inttypes.c, E1 of P69: the `0b` subject converted there, every other input handed WHOLE to the platform's own export through the private `__dss_platform_<name>` rows). imaxdiv_t is {quot, rem} of intmax_t — `long` on glibc and Apple (16 bytes, align 8, ✔ Apple run), `long long` on the UCRT; its tag `_imaxdiv_t` is DSS's (glibc and Apple leave it anonymous, the UCRT spells `_Lldiv_t` — a reserved name, so naming it is acceptance, never a program's meaning; the stdlib.json div_t precedent). wcstoimax/wcstoumax take the pair's own wchar_t — the target's ABI typedef, as stddef.json realizes it (int on x86_64 ELF and Darwin, unsigned int on aarch64 ELF, unsigned short on Windows). C99 7.8p1: <inttypes.h> includes <stdint.h> — the `includes` closure mirrors it.",
